### Bug description

Here is the output from a copilot session earlier today:

"Why it happened: When I regenerated the Prisma client in the first commit (d686b3b), the cloudflare runtime target produces bare specifiers (e.g. "../enums") rather than Deno-style explicit .ts specifiers. That output was committed as-is. Deno requires explicit extensions on all relative imports, so dropping them broke type-checking and the test runner. My subsequent patch commits recovered most of the top-level files (client.ts, commonInputTypes.ts, the internal/ files) but missed the 20 model files and the models.ts barrel — those are now fully restored."

So this is a pretty nasty bug in Prisma when using Cloudflare as the runtime in a native Deno project. We were troubleshooting an issue on Cloudflare workers, and the fix was to change runtime=deno to runtime=cloudflare. Then we regenerated everything from Prisma, and all of the .ts extensions from imports/exports in models and other generated files were removed, which causes type/lint errors.

### Severity

⚠️ Major: Breaks core functionality (e.g., migrations fail)

### Reproduction

To replicate, build a simple data model using typescript 6 on the Deno runtime, generate the prisma.schema, and you'll immediately notice linting/type issues. This is a huge problem b/c any time we make a schema change, prisma regens the schema/model files, and strips the .ts out of imports/exports, so we then have to back through the codebase and add them back. This is very inefficient.

https://github.com/jaypatrick/adblock-compiler/pull/1412. 

### Expected vs. Actual Behavior

On Deno/Typescript 6, Prisma 7 should not strip .ts extensions from imports/exports in generated code when using runtime=cloudflare

### Frequency

Consistently reproducible

### Does this occur in development or production?

Only in development (e.g., CLI tools, migrations, Prisma Studio)

### Is this a regression?

I don't know, this is our first Prisma 7 project. My guess is yes.

### Workaround

Writing a script that goes back and replaces all the stripped out .ts extensions and have it run during CI. Not really a workaround, but it's slowing us down quite a bit.

